# Era 11 — AI, Billing, Admin & Troubleshooting (V36–V39)

_Phase 2 · Concept Layer V1_

**Status: ✅ done, verified 2026-09-17**

**Implementation note:** all four topics resolved. `concepts/ai-connections-and-alie.mdx`
(V36, two-pass disclosure — checked against every checkpoint term in
`DISCLOSURE-ADDENDUM.md`'s AI-surface list, none found) and `concepts/plans-and-billing.mdx`
(V37) written new. `concepts/troubleshooting.mdx` (V38) written as the symptom-index hub,
deliberately off-template as specified — every row's link target verified to actually
contain the recovery content it points to. `concepts/administrative-commands.mdx` (V39)
resolved as a short page (option (a)), matching `/features/docs/`'s own gap 010 resolution
for `admin` (operator-only-but-mentionable, `documented-minimal` tier) — the two roadmaps'
answers for who `admin` is for now agree, per `gaps/00-index.docs` entry 2's own logged
default.

**Reversed 2026-09-17:** explicit user instruction to remove all admin documentation.
`concepts/administrative-commands.mdx` deleted, removed from `docs.json`'s Concepts group.
T34 is now option (b), omission — see `gaps/00-index.docs` entry 2 and the sibling
roadmap's `gaps/013` for the full account. 29 concept pages published, not 30.

Covers T30, T31, T33, T34: AI connections and Alie, Plans and billing, Troubleshooting,
Administrative commands. Grouped as the roadmap's final Phase 2 era because three of its
four topics require a classification or disclosure decision *before* writing, mirroring the
sibling roadmap's own Era 10–11 treatment of the same territory (admin/internal long tail,
AI surface) as a distinct risk pass rather than routine family writing.

---

## V36 — AI connections and Alie (T30)

### Goal
New concept page: supported connection types, prerequisites, model selection, sessions,
tools, approvals, workspace context, skills, usage controls, troubleshooting.

### Reuses
`cli/alie.mdx`, `cli/ai.mdx`; the sibling roadmap's own Era 11 (`11-ai-surface.docs`) note
that this family "carries its own disclosure question (agent tool wiring, MCP transport)
distinct from infrastructure commands" — same caution applies here, one layer up, and this
version does not proceed until that framing is applied.

### Implement
`concepts/ai-connections-and-alie.mdx`: "approvals" explained as the user-facing gate
(what the user sees and confirms before an action runs) without describing the approval
gate's internal routing (checkpoint terms from `DISCLOSURE-ADDENDUM.md`: `tool definition`,
`system prompt`, `context injection`, `tool-use loop`, `agent harness`, `model routing` —
all inherited directly from the sibling roadmap's own list, unchanged). "Workspace context"
and "skills" explained as what the user configures/sees available, not how context is
assembled or injected.

### Validate
**Two-read disclosure pass, mandatory** — this is the single highest-risk page in the
roadmap by topic, per both roadmaps' independent flagging of AI/agent surfaces.

### Done when
Page exists; passes two independent disclosure read-throughs.

---

## V37 — Plans and billing (T31)

### Goal
New concept page: plan limits, usage measurements, infrastructure costs vs. Beaver charges,
billing management, coupons, what happens when limits are reached.

### Reuses
`cli/plan.mdx`/`cli/plans.mdx` (pending the sibling roadmap's staleness resolution, same
caveat as V18/V32), `cli/billing.mdx`, `cli/coupon.mdx`.

### Implement
`concepts/plans-and-billing.mdx`: the infrastructure-costs-vs-Beaver-charges distinction
stated plainly since it's a common source of billing confusion, "what happens when limits
are reached" as an explicit, non-hedged consequence list (a user needs to know if a
deployment will be blocked or just flagged) — no disclosure risk here at all, this topic is
entirely business/product-facing, not mechanism-facing.

### Validate
Accuracy check against current plan structure (Invariant 1) rather than a disclosure check —
this topic's risk is being wrong or stale, not leaking mechanism.

### Done when
Page exists; plan/limit claims verified current.

---

## V38 — Troubleshooting (T33)

### Goal
New concept page — actually a **symptom-based index**, not a standard concept page shape —
covering: deployment stuck, machine offline, service unreachable, capacity exhausted,
firewall blocking traffic, configuration conflict, authentication failure.

### Reuses
Every concept page's own "Limitations" and recovery-adjacent sections written so far
(V18–V37) as link targets — this page is explicitly a hub, not new explanatory content of
its own, per Invariant 1's altitude rule (a troubleshooting index that re-explains each
feature would duplicate every page above it).

### Implement
`concepts/troubleshooting.mdx`: one row per symptom, each linking to the concept page whose
"Limitations"/recovery section actually answers it (deployment stuck → Deployments V19 +
Rollback V34; machine offline → Machine and agent operation V12; service unreachable →
Domains and routing V22 + Firewall V24; capacity exhausted → Scaling V23 + Beaver Cloud V13;
firewall blocking traffic → Firewall V24's "undo a block" section directly; configuration
conflict → Configuration V15 + State and drift V33; authentication failure → Accounts and
workspaces V9). Deviates from the standard template (noted explicitly, per the template
being a default shape, not a rigid constraint the roadmap enforces for content that
genuinely doesn't fit it).

### Validate
Every row's link target actually contains the recovery content it points to — a link-only
page with a dead-end link is worse than no page.

### Done when
Every one of the seven named symptoms resolves to a real, populated destination.

---

## V39 — Administrative commands (T34)

### Goal
Resolve the classification question Era 1 explicitly deferred: are `admin` commands public
concept-page material at all, and if so, at what disclosure level.

### Reuses
The sibling roadmap's own Era 10 (`10-admin-preflight-and-platform-ops.docs`) note: "operator-
facing; classification question." This version does not proceed independently of that
resolution — it either confirms the same answer for the concept layer or explicitly states
why the concept layer's answer differs (e.g., a one-line "these commands are for platform
operators, not typical users" note might be appropriate here even if the reference layer
documents them, since a concept page's job is user orientation).

### Implement
Either: (a) a short `concepts/administrative-commands.mdx` stating who this is for and
pointing elsewhere (most likely outcome, given the audience mismatch with every other
concept page's target reader), or (b) an explicit decision to omit T34 from the Concepts
group entirely, logged here with reasoning, satisfying Invariant 2's "never silently
dropped" without forcing a page that doesn't serve a real reader.

### Validate
Decision is consistent with whatever the sibling roadmap's Era 10 lands on for the same
commands — no contradiction between the two roadmaps about who `admin` commands are for.

### Done when
T34 has a recorded, reasoned resolution (page or explicit omission) — closes Era 11 and
Phase 2.
